  "subtitle": "Japan's videotex that faxed you the screen: pre-rendered pages, kana keypad, numeric page codes",
  "why_scout_cares": "CAPTAIN (Character and Pattern Telephone Access Information Network) was NTT's answer to European videotex — and it is the strangest of them all. Japanese kanji has 3,500+ characters, too many for an affordable character generator in a 1979-era home terminal, so CAPTAIN sent pages as pre-rendered raster images, 'using coding strategies similar to facsimile machines': the screen visibly painted in like a fax. Input was a telephone-style 12-key numeric pad with kana printed on the keys, multi-tap kana entry, and dedicated control keys for menu/page navigation. Launched November 1984, it reached only 120,000 subscribers by March 1992 — a commercially doomed but beautifully weird national project. Fills the fax-raster videotex mechanism gap; distinct from Minitel (text-based consumer videotex), Bildschirmtext (pay-per-page German videotex), and Famicom Network System (home finance).",
